Thanks Sarah, I’ll adjust to take account of that point about % of incurred costs (but will wait for any other comments before doing so).
&nbsp;
On the Post-July point:
We are 100% on the same page for the scenario if Cs win the PDD trial.
On scenario B (Cs lose PDD), I think the difference come from these two parts of your list below:

Quantum trial will not happen&nbsp;– when we were discussing this earlier in the year pre-PTR, I recall there was hesitation in the group
 about whether it would be as simple as this (or at least whether it was something we were happy to express this in these absolute terms). That’s where my concerns about the case for ‘full’ cover originate from. If we are now able (and happy) to say that an
 adverse outcome in the PDD trial would mean in practice that the T3 trial was immediately vacated that’s fine, but my understanding from previous chats was that it might not be as simple as this, and/or there could be outcomes which were effectively losses
 but which were not strictly fatal to the T3 trial going ahead? If we can’t assert that T3 would go away immediately upon an adverse PDD judgment, then we can’t say the costs will either, and so that’s where the difference of approach arises from I think.Quantum PTR, Trial Prep and Trial Phases will ever be incurred&nbsp;– if the T3 trial does get vacated at the end of July 2026, then
 you are correct that the Trial phase shouldn’t be incurred (unless some small part is used for a form of ‘consequentials’ but:


The PTR is due to be held in July 2026 I think ([47] of Quantum directions) so there’s a very real chance those costs are incurred, even if the hearing itself gets used for
 something else at the last minute; andGiven the trial starts in October with summer in between, I anticipate at least some of the trial prep costs will be incurred before the end of July. I expect some tranches
 of brief will be incurred, and there will also be at least some solicitor work (eg I can see there are trial bundle deadlines in July at [52]-[55]).

So, I think the key question is whether or not we can say with confidence that if Cs lose (/ receive an adverse costs order in) T2, that is likely to mean that
 the T3 trial gets abandoned at the end of July 2026. I’m in PG/LDs hands on that. If that’s something we can say, then yes, I can see the argument that there is unlikely to be a set of circumstances which lead to needing cover for the ‘full’ T3 period. If
 it is more complicated that that, T3 might still go ahead, that’s where we get the potential for a greater today ATE requirement.
But even if it is correct and the T3 trial would get immediately vacated, I don’t think it would necessarily mean that none of Quantum PTR or Trial Prep are incurred.
 I expect at least some of each would be incurred (and TP is obviously a sizeable sum).
