Hi James, &nbsp;Thank you for your email. This is encouraging news, and we appreciate your efforts in moving this forward.&nbsp;With regards to the additional points, I have attached a further advice on Mercedes which may be helpful. We can also arrange for Anna and a couple of the case leads to meet with Toremis tomorrow to discuss further. The team have availability between 13.30 and 17.00. &nbsp;Please let me know whether Toremis are able to accommodate.&nbsp;With regards to the cross-collateralisation point, we do have a few questions which we are hoping you can address:&nbsp;Client proceeds – please could you clarify whether cross-collateralisation has any impact on client damages? Specifically, can you confirm that it does not affect client recoveries beyond the insurer’s entitlements?Scope - how exactly will cross-collateralisation operate i.e., what types of insurer recoveries (e.g., paid claims, contingent premiums) are in scope?Existing arrangements - would there be any impact on the wrapper arrangements already in place? Can we keep them completely separate?We would also like to explore the Mosaic option you mentioned yesterday in tandem. Please could you provide some additional detail on the following points: 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Structure - the policy would need to safeguard client interests, could the policy expressly designate claimants as third-party beneficiaries? If not, would it be possible for the insurer to issue a side letter, deed of benefit, or assignment giving clients direct enforceable rights? &nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claims - it would be helpful if you could outline the claims process, including how coverage applies when the insured is not the named party to the adverse costs order.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Protections for PG – we would need to ensure that the firm is not liable if the insurer declines cover for reasons unrelated to misconduct. How can PG navigate this risk?4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claim payments - this structure could be scrutinised by the defendants and therefore it would assist if any claims monies could be directly paid to the defendant or PGs client account.&nbsp;Many thanks Helena &nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorFrom: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt; Sent: 04 November 2025 10:48To: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Hi Helena,&nbsp;I have spoken to Toremis this morning. In summary:They are likely to be able to offer terms here, subject to a few points:Merits – they pass on their thanks to Counsel for the email note. They do however require a bit more detail on merits – given Counsel are in trial, they have asked if Anna (or another case lead who is over all the matters) would be able to expand on the merits advice on a call with them?Limit requests – please could Tom also join the call as they have some supplemental queries as to how those limit requests have been calculated? Overall they want a deeper dive into the extent there are any headroom in these figures.Their likely offer:As Counsel’s note mentions, not all the cases are of uniform risk level. To address this – they have suggested a form of cross-collateralisation agreement that would sit over the eight top-up policies. Would this in principle be acceptable to PG? I note there are already the BHP reimbursement deed and the wrapper agreement that relate to the proceeds on these matters, which will need to be taken into account here. Having a cross-collateralisation agreement in place would push the pricing offered downwards.Pricing – it is likely to be relatively expensive given they are insuring these matters during a trial and so the upfront premium is likely to begin with a “2”. They would be willing to split this into say two payments (the latter of which would be payable in any event) but splitting the payments would increase the overall price e.g. 24% ROL upfront or 2x 14% ROL staggered payments (NB these numbers are just illustrative rather than the pricing that Toremis will provide). By way of comparison, where pricing can be offered during trial we have seen it be offered at 30+%.If point 1 and 2 are acceptable, would we be able to set up the requested call with Toremis this week?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116) On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.com www.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW. Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt; Sent: 04 November 2025 08:33To: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: 